  "legacyId": "f1853daf-98f6-4c06-b870-778794d75511",
  "type": "problem",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "description": "<div><p>You stand at index 0 of nums. At position i you may move forward between one and nums[i] indices; a zero leaves you unable to move. Return whether you can reach the last index, including when you start there.</p><p>Choosing a shorter jump can be useful if it leads to a position with greater reach.</p><p><strong>Example 1</strong></p><pre>Input: nums = [2,3,1,1,4]\nOutput: true</pre><p>Explanation: Index 1 can reach index 4.</p><p><strong>Example 2</strong></p><pre>Input: nums = [3,2,1,0,4]\nOutput: false</pre><p>Explanation: Every path ends at the zero at index 3 before reaching index 4.</p><p><strong>Example 3</strong></p><pre>Input: nums = [0]\nOutput: true</pre><p>Explanation: You already occupy the destination.</p><p><strong>Constraints</strong></p><ul><li>1 ≤ nums.length ≤ 10,000.</li><li>0 ≤ nums[i] ≤ 100,000.</li></ul></div>",
  "templates": {
    "python": "class Solution:\n    def canJump(self, nums: List[int]) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Return the requested result.\"\"\"\n        pass\n",
    "javascript": "var canJump = function(nums) {\n    // your code here\n};\n"
  },
  "exampleTestCases": [
    {
      "inputs": [
        "[2,3,1,1,4]"
      ],
      "expectedOutput": "true",
      "explanation": "Index 1 can reach index 4."
    },
    {
      "inputs": [
        "[3,2,1,0,4]"
      ],
      "expectedOutput": "false",
      "explanation": "Every path ends at the zero at index 3 before reaching index 4."
    },
    {
      "inputs": [
        "[0]"
      ],
      "expectedOutput": "true",
      "explanation": "You already occupy the destination."
    }
